# **3. Architecture — Critical Rules**

*These rules must never be violated. If you are unsure, check the repo before writing code.*

## **One-way import rule**

schema.js → data.js → module files → app.js. Module JS files never import from each other. If a module needs data, it goes through data.js. This is enforced — do not break it.

## **Data files (all in OneDrive/DPC-Hub/Data)**

| **File** | **Key** | **Notes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| dpc-manifest.json | — | Master index. Created on first run. References all data files. |
| data-areas.json | areaCode | The join key across ALL systems. 35 areas. Needs correction. |
| data-calendar.json | entryId | Calendar entries and tasks |
| data-staff.json | staffId | Staff profiles |
| data-afi.json | afiId | AFI loops (Loops module) |
| data-reflections.json | reflectionId | Reflections |
| data-templates.json | templateId | Teach Meet plans, coaching Qs, agendas |
| data-cpd.json | — | CPD log, planned training, delivered CPD |
| data-digital-leads.json | dlId | Digital Lead profiles |
| data-current-focus.json | focusId | Current focus areas |

## **Design token rule**

All colours, spacing, typography, and shadows are defined as CSS custom properties in css/design.css only. No hardcoded values anywhere else. No inline style attributes in HTML. Every colour reference uses var(--color-xxx).

## **WCAG 2.2 AA is a build gate**

Every module must meet WCAG 2.2 AA. Minimum contrast: 4.5:1 normal text, 3:1 large text. Minimum touch target: 44px. All interactive elements have visible focus rings. Colour is never used as the sole means of conveying information — always paired with text labels or aria-labels.

## **File delivery conventions**

- Always state exactly which files changed and which did not.

- Present each changed file individually for download — never zip unless explicitly asked.

- Validate JS syntax with node --check before delivering.

- Never rebuild files that have not changed.

# **4. Pedagogical Framework**

This system is grounded in specific frameworks. References to these must be accurate.

## **LRA Taxonomy 26/27**

7 categories, 30 themes. This is the backbone for all observation, AFI identification, and coaching. It is hardcoded in schema.js as LRA_TAXONOMY. Never invent LRA themes.

| **Category** | **Themes** |
| --- | --- |
| CAT1 — Planning and Sequencing | LI, BB, SP, LL |
| CAT2 — Inclusive TLA & SEND | PL, AR, ARD, LL2, NS |
| CAT3 — Inclusive Learning Environment | LE, LED, RL, PR, BR, EAS |
| CAT4 — Modelling and Professional Language | PC, LM, SSL, ISV |
| CAT5 — Engagement, Collaboration and Independence | AL, AE, SS, CL, LI2, AT |
| CAT6 — Assessment, Feedback and Progress | CU, EQ, EF, EA, AP |
| CAT7 — Personal Development and Wellbeing | RTL, RTW, RFL, FS |

## **Hyper focus areas 2025-26 (college priority)**

Three themes are surfaced prominently in all observation tools as the college-wide priority for 2025-26:

- ARD — Accessible by Design (Accessible Resources Digital, CAT2)

- LED — Digital Learning Environment (CAT3)

- AT — Assistive Technology (CAT5)

These appear as pre-checked prominent chips in DevObs, Learning Walk, and Quick Capture. They are defined in schema.js as HYPER_FOCUS.

## **RAG dimensions (8)**

Staff Capability · HoA Leadership · Infrastructure & Devices · Digital Skills Assessment · Curriculum Integration · Learner Readiness · Accessibility & Inclusion · Digital Lead Engagement. Defined in schema.js as RAG_DIMENSIONS. Scored 1–5 with text labels (never colour alone).

## **ETF DTPF stages**

Stages 1–5 (Explore, Adopt, Adapt, Create, Innovate). Used in staff profiles and DevObs context.

## **AFI severity labels (exact — do not change)**

- Strength

- Areas to Strengthen

- Areas for Immediate Improvement

# **5. Seed Data — Needs Correction**

*The current data/areas-seed.json uses placeholder area names. Graeme will provide the correct list. When he does, produce both: (1) corrected areas-seed.json for the GitHub repo, and (2) corrected data-areas.json for OneDrive. Area codes (3 letters) are the join key across all systems — if codes change, all linked data becomes orphaned. Confirm codes carefully before producing the file.*

Each area record has this structure:

{ "areaCode": "BUI", "areaName": "Business & Industry", "campus": "Weston", "hoaName": "", "digitalLeadId": null, "pyramidLevel": "foundations", "ragDimensions": {}, "healthChecks": [], "activityLog": [], "afiRefs": [], "actionPoints": [], "staffRefs": [], "notes": "", "lastUpdated": "" }

The 35 placeholder codes currently in the repo (may need renaming): ACA, AHC, ART, BEA, BUI, CAR, CHI, COG, CON, CRE, CRI, DIG, EAR, ELE, ENG, ENT, FLO, HAI, HBH, HOS, INC, LAW, MAT, MED, MUS, NUR, OUT, PER, PHO, PLA, PUB, SCH, SCI, SPO, TRA

# **6. Phase 7 — What to Build**

## **6.1 js/reports.js**

Five report types, each producing a structured summary for a different audience. Reports are rendered as HTML within the Hub (printable) and optionally downloadable as Word documents. They pull live data from window.DPC_DATA.

| **Report** | **Audience** | **Frequency** | **Key content** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Neil fortnightly | Neil Davies (AP Quality) | Fortnightly | Activities this period, open loops by severity, areas visited, reflections received, upcoming actions |
| DL area briefing | Digital Lead for a specific area | On demand | That area's RAG scores, open loops, recent activities, resources created, next steps |
| AP area briefing | AP responsible for an area | On demand | Area RAG summary, open loops, Health Check scores, staff development progress |
| HoA area briefing | Head of Area | On demand | Accessible summary of digital development progress, no jargon, what's working and what needs attention |
| Performance review | Self / line manager | Termly | Full activity log, AFI lifecycle data, reflections received, CPD log, AI-generated narrative summary of impact |

## **6.2 hub-lite.html**

A mobile-first companion page. Separate HTML file. No Hub navigation — clean single-column layout. Three surfaces:

- Jobs Board surface — horizontal scrollable row of post-it cards (title, due date, status dot only). Read-only.

- Calendar surface — list of events for today and next 7 days. Read-only.

- Quick Capture — two large buttons: "+ Note" and "+ Meeting shell". Tap, type, save. Writes to Supabase immediately.

Supabase credentials go in js/config.js (SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_ANON_KEY). The table is hub_lite_captures with columns: id, captured_at, type (note/meeting), content (JSON), synced_to_hub (boolean).

## **6.3 js/supabase-sync.js**

Reads pending captures from Supabase (synced_to_hub = false). Shows them in the Hub as a notification panel. Graeme reviews each one and clicks Commit to Hub — which writes it to the appropriate OneDrive data file and marks it synced_to_hub = true in Supabase.

# **7. Key Design Decisions — Do Not Revisit**

*These decisions were made deliberately. Do not reopen them unless Graeme explicitly asks.*

| **Decision** | **Rationale** |
| --- | --- |
| No framework, no build step | College network may block CDN requests. Plain HTML/CSS/JS loads from GitHub Pages reliably. No npm run build means Graeme can edit files directly. |
| File System Access API for OneDrive | Keeps sensitive data off the internet. No backend. No authentication complexity. Weekly reconnect is a deliberate security feature. |
| Area code as join key | Stable identifier across all data files. Never changes once set. Changing it orphans all linked data. |
| Heuristic rules (not AI) for coaching questions | Deterministic, auditable, no API dependency for core coaching logic. AI is only used for Health Check extraction where pattern recognition adds real value. |
| WalkThrus excluded by name | Tom Sherrington's WalkThrus are excluded from the resource library by design. Do not add them. |
| Reflections anonymous at area level | Staff are identified in their profile but reflections are reported anonymously at area level. This is a deliberate trust-building design. |
| Templates are versioned | Each edit creates a new version. Instances reference their version. Old instances are not broken by template updates. |
| Anthropic API called directly from browser for HC | GitHub Pages cannot have a backend. The API call uses the anthropic-dangerous-direct-browser-access header. This is acceptable for a private single-user tool on a private college network. |

# **8. Working Style and Conventions**

## **How Graeme communicates**

- Direct and collegial. Plain English. No unnecessary formality.

- He sends screenshots of what he sees. Read them carefully before responding.

- He tests each phase and reports results. A PASS means move on. A FAIL means he describes what went wrong.

- He will say "ready" when he wants you to build. Do not ask unnecessary clarifying questions before starting.

## **How you should communicate**

- State what you're building before you build it.

- State exactly which files changed and which did not — every time.

- Do not produce documentation when asked for code. Do not produce code when asked for documentation.

- When you fix a bug, explain the root cause in one sentence and the fix in one sentence.

- Never use governance review language — no October review, no 3-month, 6-month, 9-month, 12-month reviews, no pilot framing. This programme is organic.

## **Build discipline**

- Validate JS syntax (node --check) before delivering any JS file.

- Present files individually for download — never zip unless asked.

- Never touch files outside the agreed scope for that session.

- If something is ambiguous, flag it before building — not after.

- Read screenshots carefully. The console errors tell you what's wrong.

